// Stories for the matrix: STORIES["<civ id>:<activity>"] = "…". See cultures/README.md.
Object.assign(STORIES, {
  // ——— Goths ———
  "goths:hunt": "Imagined: In the oak and pine country along the Vistula, Gothic hunters move at first light with spears and lean hounds, following the slots of a red deer through the wet ferns. The lyre-player stays home; out here the only music is a cow-horn call between the men. A good stag means meat for the long hall and a hide for winter, and the hunters thank the gods quietly before the knife goes in.",
  "goths:battle": "On 9 August 378, near Adrianople, the Gothic wagon-ring of Fritigern waited in the heat while the Roman army of Emperor Valens marched up thirsty and tired. Then the Gothic cavalry under Alatheus and Saphrax came back from foraging and struck like lightning. The Roman lines were crushed so tightly that men could not lift their swords. Valens died and was never found. For the Goths, who had crossed the Danube as starving refugees, it was the day they stopped begging.",
  "goths:gather": "In the fourth century the bishop Wulfila (Ulfilas), himself a Goth, made letters for his people and translated the Bible into Gothic. Picture a congregation gathered by the Danube, farmers and warriors who had never seen their own speech written, hearing the Lord's Prayer read aloud in their mother tongue: Atta unsar thu in himinam. The Silver Bible, the Codex Argenteus in Uppsala, still keeps his words in silver ink on purple vellum.",
  "goths:ritual": "Around 370, the Gothic judge Athanaric held to the old gods. A wooden image was drawn on a wagon from house to house, and each family had to come out, bow and offer to it, asking the gods to keep the people whole in a dangerous time. Saba, a Christian Goth, refused and was drowned in the river Musaeus in 372. Both sides believed the future of the Goths depended on whom they honoured, and neither took the question lightly.",
  "goths:rain": "In 376 tens of thousands of Goths, driven west by the Huns, gathered on the north bank of the Danube and waited for Rome to let them cross. Days stretched into weeks. Food ran out; the historian Ammianus says Roman officers sold them dogs to eat, one dog for one Gothic child taken as a slave. They watched the far bank and the sky, waiting for the world to open to them, and their patience slowly turned to anger.",
  "goths:flood": "When Emperor Valens finally allowed the Tervingi across the Danube in 376, the river was swollen with rain. Ammianus Marcellinus writes that the Goths crossed day and night in boats, on rafts and in hollowed tree trunks, and that many who tried to swim against the strong current were swept away and drowned. Families on the far bank counted heads as the boats came in, knowing some would never arrive.",
  "goths:wind": "In the 250s and 260s, Gothic and Herul war-bands took to the Black Sea in light boats and raided Trebizond, the Bosporus and even Athens. Zosimus writes that some fleets were caught by storms and scattered. Imagine the crews clinging to the gunwales as black waves broke over them, far from any shore they knew, with only their oars and the gods to hold on to until the wind dropped.",
  "goths:fire": "On 24 August 410, Alaric's Visigoths entered Rome through the Salarian Gate, the first foreign army inside the city in eight hundred years. Buildings near the gate burned, among them the house and gardens of the historian Sallust. Alaric, a Christian, ordered that the churches of Peter and Paul be spared as sanctuaries. For three days the Goths, long treated as refugees and mercenaries, held the city that had despised them.",
  "goths:thunder": "Imagined: Before most Goths became Christian, thunder belonged to Thunraz, the sky god who struck at giants with his hammer. When a summer storm rolls over a Gothic village on the Black Sea steppe, the women pull the children inside and the old men stand in the doorways watching the lightning walk across the grass, reading in its direction whether the year will bring war or a good harvest.",
  "goths:birth": "The historian Jordanes tells that around 454, on the very day the Ostrogothic king Valamir drove off a band of Huns, a messenger rode to his brother Thiudimer with news that his concubine Ereleuva had borne a son. The boy was Theodoric, later called the Great, who would rule Italy for thirty years. Nobody at that cradle knew it; they only saw a victory and a birth on one day and hoped it was a sign.",
  "goths:death": "Alaric died suddenly in Calabria in 410, only months after taking Rome. Jordanes writes that his Goths turned the river Busento from its course, dug a grave in the riverbed, buried their king there with many treasures, and then let the water flow back over him. The captives who did the digging were killed, so that no one would ever find the place. The river still runs over it, and no one ever has.",
  // ——— Teutons (Teutonic Order) ———
  "teutons:hunt": "The Teutonic Knights of Prussia were famous falconers. From the Baltic coast and as far as Livonia they caught gyrfalcons and peregrines, trained them at Marienburg, and sent them every year as diplomatic gifts to kings and princes across Europe. Picture a brother-knight in his white mantle with the black cross, a hooded falcon on his glove, riding out over the frozen marsh, while the bird that will soon win friends in Paris or London learns to stoop on a heron.",
  "teutons:battle": "On 15 July 1410, at Grunwald (Tannenberg), Grand Master Ulrich von Jungingen sent two naked swords to King Jagiełło and Grand Duke Vytautas, mocking them for waiting. The Polish-Lithuanian army answered, and by evening the Order's host was broken. Ulrich himself died in the charge, with most of the Order's leading brothers. The knights had come to Prussia believing God marched with them; that night many survivors wondered what they had done to lose His favour.",
  "teutons:gather": "In the late fourteenth century the Order held the Ehrentisch, the Table of Honour, for guest crusaders who came to Prussia from England, France and the Empire. Before a campaign into Lithuania, the most deserving knights were seated at a feast under banners, heralds read out their deeds, and each received a badge with the motto Honneur vainc tout. Chaucer's Knight had sat at that table, the poet says: ful ofte tyme he hadde the bord bigonne in Pruce.",
  "teutons:ritual": "When a Grand Master died, the brothers gathered in the chapter house of Marienburg to choose a successor. Thirteen electors were chosen, symbolising Christ and the twelve apostles, and they voted after Mass and prayer to the Virgin Mary, the Order's patroness. The knights believed the choice was not truly theirs but God's, and they waited in silence for the bells that would announce whom heaven had given them to lead.",
  "teutons:rain": "The Order's raids into Lithuania, the Reisen, depended on weather. A winter raid needed hard frost so that horses and wagons could cross the marshes and rivers; a summer raid needed dry ground. When a thaw came early or autumn rains drowned the roads, guest knights from all over Europe sat in Königsberg for weeks, drinking and dicing, watching the sky and waiting for the world to freeze so their crusade could begin.",
  "teutons:flood": "The Order turned the swampy Vistula delta, the Żuławy, into farmland by building dikes and drainage channels and inviting Dutch and German settlers. Every spring the Vistula and Nogat rose with meltwater and ice, and the villagers walked the dikes day and night with shovels and sandbags. When a dike broke, the water poured across fields that had taken generations to win, and the brothers of the Order rode out to help close the gap.",
  "teutons:wind": "Imagined: A late-autumn gale comes off the Baltic onto the Curonian Spit. A cog carrying salt, cloth and two young guest knights to Memel is driven along the narrow sand barrier, its sail torn away. The crew pray to the Virgin and throw cargo overboard. By morning the ship lies broken on the beach, and fishermen of the Order's coast drag survivors through the surf, wrapped in their cloaks with the black cross.",
  "teutons:fire": "After Grunwald in 1410, the Polish-Lithuanian army marched on Marienburg. Heinrich von Plauen, a mere commander who took charge of the defence, ordered the town below the castle burned so that the enemy could find no shelter in it. The Order's own people watched their houses go up in flames. The castle held through an eight-week siege, and Heinrich was elected Grand Master in thanks for saving the Order's heart.",
  "teutons:thunder": "Imagined: In a newly conquered Prussian village, the old people still remember the sacred oaks of Perkūnas, the thunder god, which the knights had felled. One summer evening a storm breaks and lightning splits the church tower. The German priest rings the bell and prays; the Prussian grandmothers mutter that the old god has not forgotten. Both are frightened, and both are listening to the same thunder.",
  "teutons:birth": "Imagined: The knights were monks sworn to celibacy, but the land they ruled filled with settlers under Kulm law. In a new village near Thorn, a peasant wife labours through the night while neighbours light a candle to the Virgin. The midwife lifts a healthy boy into the lamplight. His father hopes he will inherit the fields; his mother hopes he will never ride on a Reise into Lithuania.",
  "teutons:death": "The Grand Masters of the Teutonic Order were buried in St Anne's Chapel beneath the castle church at Marienburg, under stone slabs carved with their names. After Grunwald, King Jagiełło had the body of Ulrich von Jungingen sent back with honour, and it was laid there among his predecessors. The brothers sang the office of the dead in Latin, a sombre chant for a knight whom they believed had died as a soldier of Christ.",
  // ——— Germans ———
  "germans:hunt": "On 3 November, St Hubert's Day, German hunters hold the Hubertusjagd. Hubert, the legend says, was a reckless hunter who saw a stag with a shining cross between its antlers and turned to God. So the hunt begins with a Hubertus Mass and the brass of the hunting horns, and ends with the Strecke: the game laid out in rows on fir branches, a sprig placed in each mouth as a last bite, and the horns blowing a signal of respect for every animal taken.",
  "germans:battle": "At Pavia on 24 February 1525, the Landsknechte of Georg von Frundsberg, father of the Landsknechte, fought for Emperor Charles V against King Francis I of France. Pikemen in slashed and puffed clothes, drums and fifes beside them, pushed through the park walls in the morning mist. By noon the French army was broken and the King of France was a prisoner. Frundsberg's men, unpaid for months, sang of it for years.",
  "germans:gather": "On 12 October 1810 Crown Prince Ludwig of Bavaria married Princess Therese of Saxe-Hildburghausen, and the citizens of Munich were invited to a meadow outside the city gate for horse races in their honour. The meadow was named Theresienwiese, Therese's meadow, and the party was held again the next year, and the next. The Oktoberfest grew from that wedding: brass bands, long tables, tents, and a whole city gathering to drink and sing together.",
  "germans:ritual": "On the evening of 17 April 1521, Martin Luther stood before Emperor Charles V at the Diet of Worms and was asked to recant his books. He asked for a night to think. He spent it, by tradition, in prayer, knowing that his answer could mean the stake. The next day he said that his conscience was captive to the Word of God and he could not go against it. The famous words Here I stand were probably added later; the prayer was real.",
  "germans:rain": "1816 was the year without a summer. The eruption of Tambora in Indonesia had filled the sky with ash, and in Germany it rained almost without end. Grain rotted in the fields, horses starved for lack of oats, and families watched the grey clouds week after week, waiting for a sun that never came. In Mannheim, Karl Drais built a two-wheeled running machine, the ancestor of the bicycle, partly to replace the horses that had died.",
  "germans:flood": "On the night of 16 to 17 February 1962, a North Sea storm surge broke the dikes of Hamburg. Water poured into the low districts of Wilhelmsburg while people slept; 315 died. The young police senator Helmut Schmidt, overstepping his authority, called in the Bundeswehr and NATO helicopters to pluck families from rooftops. Hamburgers still remember the cold, the darkness, and the neighbours who rowed through the streets to save strangers.",
  "germans:wind": "On 16 January 1362, a great southwesterly storm drove the North Sea over the coasts of Frisia and Schleswig. The Grote Mandrenke, the great drowning of men, killed tens of thousands and swallowed the trading town of Rungholt, which vanished beneath the Wadden Sea. Fishermen later told of hearing its church bells ringing under the water on quiet nights, a legend that kept the memory of the storm alive for centuries.",
  "germans:fire": "On 5 May 1842 a fire broke out in a cigar maker's house on the Deichstraße in Hamburg. A dry wind spread it for four days; the old town burned, the churches of St Nicholas and St Peter fell, and the town hall was blown up to stop the flames. About twenty thousand people lost their homes. Hamburg rebuilt with a modern sewer system and wide streets, but the people who watched it burn never forgot the red sky.",
  "germans:thunder": "On 2 July 1505, the law student Martin Luther was walking back to Erfurt when a thunderstorm caught him near the village of Stotternheim. A bolt of lightning struck close by and threw him to the ground. In terror he cried out: Help, Saint Anne, I will become a monk! Two weeks later, against his father's wishes, he entered the Augustinian monastery in Erfurt. A storm in a field changed the history of Christianity.",
  "germans:birth": "On 10 November 1483 a son was born to Hans and Margarethe Luder, a mining family in Eisleben. The next day, St Martin's Day, they carried him to the church of St Peter and Paul to be baptised and named him Martin, after the saint of the day. Infant death was common, so baptism could not wait. His father hoped the boy would become a lawyer and lift the family; nobody imagined what he would actually do.",
  "germans:death": "Protestant Germans of the 16th and 17th centuries printed Leichenpredigten, funeral sermons, for the dead of middling and noble families. Each booklet held the sermon preached at the grave, a life story, the illness and the last words, and poems by friends; some two hundred and fifty thousand survive. Families read them aloud for comfort, and today they are among the best sources for how ordinary Germans lived, loved and faced death with hope of resurrection.",
  // ——— Holy Roman Empire ———
  "holy-roman-empire:hunt": "Emperor Frederick II, Stupor Mundi, wrote around 1240 a great book on hunting with birds, De arte venandi cum avibus. He had watched falcons himself for decades, measured their wings, and dared to correct Aristotle when observation proved him wrong. Imagine the emperor riding out from his castle in Apulia, a gyrfalcon on his fist, reasoning about flight as the bird climbs into the wind, half a hunter and half a scientist.",
  "holy-roman-empire:battle": "On 10 August 955, at the Lechfeld near Augsburg, King Otto I faced the Hungarian horse-archers who had raided the German lands for fifty years. He carried the Holy Lance, believed to hold a nail from the Cross, and the army swore peace among themselves before battle. The Hungarians were broken and their leaders hanged. Otto was hailed as emperor by his troops on the field, and seven years later Rome crowned him.",
  "holy-roman-empire:gather": "On 7 August 936 the nobles of the realm gathered at Charlemagne's chapel in Aachen to see Otto I crowned. He was anointed, girded with a sword, and led up to the marble throne of Charlemagne on the gallery, where all could see him. At the feast afterwards, the dukes of Lotharingia, Franconia, Swabia and Bavaria served him as chamberlain, steward, cupbearer and marshal. The kingdom gathered in one hall around one man.",
  "holy-roman-empire:ritual": "Hildegard of Bingen, abbess on the Rhine, saw visions from childhood and recorded them in her book Scivias, Know the Ways, finished in 1151. Popes, bishops and Emperor Frederick Barbarossa wrote to her for counsel about the future. She warned him sternly against supporting antipopes. In her convent the nuns sang her own chants, believing that through her God was speaking about what was to come for the Church and the Empire.",
  "holy-roman-empire:rain": "In January 1077, Emperor Henry IV, excommunicated by Pope Gregory VII, crossed the Alps in deep winter and came to the castle of Canossa. For three days he stood outside the gate barefoot in the snow, in a penitent's woollen shirt, waiting for the Pope to receive him. Inside, Gregory waited too, deciding. On the fourth day the gates opened. Going to Canossa still means, in German, humbling yourself and waiting for the other side to relent.",
  "holy-roman-empire:flood": "In July 1342, after heavy rains, the St Mary Magdalene's flood struck the heart of the Empire. The Main, Rhine, Danube, Moselle and Elbe all burst their banks; in Frankfurt the water reached the cathedral, in Würzburg the bridge was destroyed, and in Cologne boats rowed over the city walls. Topsoil was washed off the hills across Central Europe. Flood marks from that summer, still visible on old walls, stand higher than any since.",
  "holy-roman-empire:wind": "At Pentecost 1184, Emperor Frederick Barbarossa held a great court festival at Mainz to knight his sons, with tens of thousands of guests camped in a tent city on the Rhine meadows. Then a violent storm swept the camp. A newly built wooden church and several houses collapsed, killing a number of people. Chroniclers took it as a warning from heaven against pride, and the celebration that was meant to show the Empire's glory ended in grief.",
  "holy-roman-empire:fire": "In 1689, during the War of the Palatine Succession, French troops burned Speyer and its cathedral, the burial church of the Salian emperors. Soldiers broke open the imperial tombs looking for treasure and scattered the bones of emperors from Conrad II to Henry V. The people of Speyer, driven out of the city, watched from the fields as the great Romanesque nave burned. The Empire could not even protect its own emperors' graves.",
  "holy-roman-empire:thunder": "Across the medieval Empire, when a thunderstorm approached, the sexton climbed the church tower and rang the bells. This Wetterläuten was believed to drive away storms and hail, since church bells were blessed and many were inscribed with words like fulgura frango, I break the lightning. Sometimes the lightning struck the tower and killed the bell-ringer instead. Still the villagers rang, standing between their harvest and the sky with nothing but prayer and bronze.",
  "holy-roman-empire:birth": "On 26 December 1194, the Empress Constance, aged forty and married for years without a child, gave birth to a son at Jesi in Italy. Because many doubted that a woman her age could truly be the mother, she had a tent raised in the market square and gave birth before the matrons of the town, then nursed the baby in public. The boy was Frederick II, who grew up to amaze, alarm and outrage all of Christendom.",
  "holy-roman-empire:death": "On 10 June 1190, on the Third Crusade, Emperor Frederick Barbarossa drowned crossing the river Saleph in Anatolia. His army, stunned, boiled his body to preserve it: his flesh was buried in Antioch, his bones meant for Jerusalem but lost along the way. Because no grave held him, Germans told that he was not dead but asleep inside the Kyffhäuser mountain, his red beard growing through the stone table, waiting to return when the Empire needed him.",
  // ——— Order of the Dragon ———
  "order-of-the-dragon:hunt": "Imagined: In the beech forests of the Southern Carpathians, a Wallachian boyar who wears the Order's badge, a dragon coiled in a ring with a red cross on its back, rides out with his huntsmen after a wild boar. Hounds bay in the ravine, a horn sounds, and the boar breaks cover. The hunt is sport, but it is also rehearsal: every man present knows the Ottoman frontier lies just beyond the Danube.",
  "order-of-the-dragon:battle": "On the night of 17 June 1462, Vlad III Dracula, son of a Knight of the Dragon, led a few thousand horsemen into the camp of Sultan Mehmed II near Târgoviște, hoping to kill the conqueror of Constantinople in his tent. Torches, trumpets and panic filled the dark; the raid struck the wrong tent and Mehmed survived. Days later the Ottoman army found the forest of the impaled outside Târgoviște, and the Sultan, shaken, turned his army home.",
  "order-of-the-dragon:gather": "On 12 December 1408, King Sigismund of Hungary and his queen Barbara of Celje founded the Society of the Dragon with twenty-four noble lords. They swore to defend the cross against the Ottomans and to stand by the king and his family. Members wore the dragon badge on their clothes and their tombs. Imagine the great hall at Buda, banners and candles, the knights kneeling one by one to take the oath, their hands between the king's.",
  "order-of-the-dragon:ritual": "In February 1431, at Nuremberg, Emperor Sigismund invested Vlad, a Wallachian prince in exile, as a Knight of the Dragon. From then on he was called Vlad Dracul, Vlad the Dragon. The ceremony took place in the presence of the clergy, with Mass and oaths sworn on the Gospels. Vlad asked God to give him back his father's throne and the strength to hold the Danube; within five years he ruled Wallachia.",
  "order-of-the-dragon:rain": "After 1462 Vlad III Dracula was held by King Matthias Corvinus of Hungary for about twelve years, first at Visegrád and later at Pest. The once-feared prince of Wallachia waited, season after season, while his enemies ruled his land and the Ottomans grew stronger. Stories say he caught mice in his cell and impaled them on sticks. In 1474 he was finally freed, and in 1476 he rode home to take his throne back one last time.",
  "order-of-the-dragon:flood": "Imagined: Spring meltwater from the Carpathians swells the Argeș and the Danube until the river spreads miles wide across the Wallachian plain. A frontier fortress manned by the Order's allies stands with water lapping at its gate, and the garrison watches the far bank. They are not sure whether to be grateful: the flood that drowns their fields also keeps the Ottoman raiders on the other side, at least until the water falls.",
  "order-of-the-dragon:wind": "Imagined: An autumn storm howls down through the Bran pass between Transylvania and Wallachia. A small company of riders carrying letters from the Order to the voivode in Târgoviște shelters under an overhang as rain lashes the cliffs and pines crack in the forest. Their captain fingers the dragon badge on his cloak and murmurs a prayer to St George, the dragon-slayer, whose own emblem they wear in reverse.",
  "order-of-the-dragon:fire": "In the winter of 1461 to 1462, Vlad III crossed the frozen Danube and burned Ottoman forts, towns and villages along the river from Zimnicea to the Black Sea. In a letter to King Matthias dated 11 February 1462 he listed the dead place by place, more than twenty thousand. For the Christian princes it was proof he would not bow; for the people along the Danube it was a winter of smoke, ash and terror.",
  "order-of-the-dragon:thunder": "Imagined: Thunder rolls over the walls of Poenari, the fortress on the cliff above the Argeș river that Vlad III had rebuilt. The guards on the parapet watch lightning strike the opposite peak and cross themselves. Below, in the chapel, the priest is singing the vespers, and the old soldiers say God speaks in thunder to remind princes that even the strongest castle is small under the sky.",
  "order-of-the-dragon:birth": "Around 1431, in the hilltop town of Sighișoara in Transylvania, a second son was born to Vlad, the exiled Wallachian prince who that same year joined the Order of the Dragon. The house on the town square where tradition says he was born still stands. The child was Vlad III, later called Dracula, son of the Dragon. His father hoped he would win back Wallachia; no one could foresee the dark legend he would leave.",
  "order-of-the-dragon:death": "At the turn of 1476 to 1477, Vlad III was killed in battle against the Ottomans near Bucharest, perhaps betrayed by his own boyars. His head was sent to Constantinople and displayed as proof. His body, tradition says, was buried by the monks of Snagov monastery on an island in a lake, though the grave has never been certainly found. For his people he was both a terror and a defender, and they mourned him as both.",
  // ——— Vikings ———
  "vikings:hunt": "Around 890 a Norwegian chieftain named Ohthere visited King Alfred of Wessex and described his home in Hålogaland, the far north. He told how he had sailed round the North Cape to hunt walrus, horse-whales he called them, for their tusks of fine ivory and their hides, cut into ship's ropes. He boasted that he and five others killed sixty in two days. Alfred's scribes wrote it down, and his voice survives in Old English.",
  "vikings:battle": "On 25 September 1066, at Stamford Bridge, King Harald Hardrada of Norway was caught by surprise by the English army of Harold Godwinson. The Norse had left their mail-coats with the ships. The chronicle tells that one huge Norseman held the bridge alone with an axe, cutting down forty men, until an Englishman floated under the bridge in a tub and speared him from below. Hardrada fell with an arrow in his throat, fighting, as a king should.",
  "vikings:gather": "Around 930, the settlers of Iceland founded the Althing, an open-air assembly at Thingvellir, a rift valley between cliffs. Every June, chieftains and farmers rode in from all over the island and camped in turf booths for two weeks. The Lawspeaker stood on the Law Rock and recited one third of the law from memory each year. Disputes were judged, marriages arranged, news traded, and an island with no king governed itself.",
  "vikings:ritual": "The Saga of Erik the Red tells of a hungry winter in Greenland around 1000. At Herjolfsnes the farmers invited Thorbjorg, a seeress, a völva. She came in a blue cloak, a hood of black lambskin lined with white catskin, and sat on a high seat. A young woman, Gudrid, sang the old warding songs for her, and spirits gathered. Then Thorbjorg foretold that the famine would end with spring, and gave Gudrid a great destiny far away.",
  "vikings:rain": "Imagined: At the mouth of a Norwegian fjord, a crew bound west for Iceland waits for byr, the fair wind. Days of rain and contrary wind pass. The ship is loaded with cattle, seed corn and families; the skipper watches clouds and birds, and the old men read the swell. Nobody sails against the sea. When at last the wind swings east, they push off within the hour, leaving the old country behind.",
  "vikings:flood": "Imagined: In southern Iceland the glacier Mýrdalsjökull hides the volcano Katla. One day the ground shakes, and a jökulhlaup, a glacier burst, pours across the sandur plain, a wall of meltwater, ice blocks and black sand. Farmers on the plain have minutes to drive their cattle to high ground. Afterwards the land is a grey waste where farms stood, and the families start again, as Icelanders always have.",
  "vikings:wind": "In 986, the Greenlanders' Saga tells, Bjarni Herjólfsson sailed from Iceland to join his father in Greenland. A great storm and fog drove his ship far off course for days. When it cleared, the crew saw a wooded land with low hills, not Greenland at all. Bjarni refused to land and turned north. He had been the first European to see America, and his men grumbled that he had not even gone ashore.",
  "vikings:fire": "Njáls saga tells how, around 1011, a band of enemies led by Flosi surrounded the farm of Njál at Bergþórshvoll in Iceland and set it on fire. Flosi offered Njál's wife Bergthora the chance to leave. She refused: I was given to Njál young, and I promised him that one fate should befall us both. The old couple lay down in their bed with their little grandson between them, under an ox-hide, and the house burned over them.",
  "vikings:thunder": "The Saga of Erik the Red tells that in Vinland, during a hungry winter, the old hunter Thorhall went missing. He was found on a crag, staring at the sky and muttering. Soon after, a whale washed ashore, and Thorhall said: Did not Red-beard prove stronger than your Christ? That was my payment for the poem I made to Thor. Many Norse wore little Thor's hammers, Mjölnir, as amulets, even after they were baptised.",
  "vikings:birth": "In the Norse world a newborn was laid on the floor until the father decided whether to raise it. If he lifted it up, the child was sprinkled with water, ausa vatni, and given a name, often of a dead kinsman whose luck might return in the child. From that moment the child belonged to the family. Every family hoped the boy might become a great skald or chieftain, and feared what the Norns had already spun.",
  "vikings:death": "In 961 King Hákon the Good fell at the battle of Fitjar, defending Norway from the sons of Erik Bloodaxe. The skald Eyvindr composed Hákonarmál: the valkyries Göndul and Skögul ride over the field, choose Hákon, and lead him to Valhalla, where Odin's heroes welcome him. His men grieved, but they also sang. For the Norse, a king who died with a sword in his hand had not lost; his name would live as long as the poem.",
  // ——— Swedes ———
  "swedes:hunt": "Every autumn, in early October, Sweden's älgjakt, the moose hunt, begins. In the northern forests whole villages join the jaktlag, the hunting team: some stand on the pass, others walk with a barking elkhound through the spruce and bog. Schools in the north have long given a moose-hunting holiday. When the bull falls, the hunters gather round a fire with coffee, and the meat is shared out to every family through the winter.",
  "swedes:battle": "On 6 November 1632, at Lützen in Saxony, King Gustavus Adolphus led his cavalry into a thick fog against Wallenstein's imperial army. He rode ahead and was lost in the mist; shot several times, he fell from his horse and died alone. His riderless horse, bleeding, ran back to the Swedish lines. The army, enraged, fought on and won the field by nightfall. Sweden won the battle and lost the Lion of the North.",
  "swedes:gather": "At midsommar, the Friday nearest the summer solstice, Swedes gather in meadows across the country. They cover a tall pole, the midsommarstång, with birch leaves and flowers, raise it together, and dance around it to fiddles, hopping like little frogs in the song Små grodorna. Herring, new potatoes, strawberries and schnapps follow. In the north the sun hardly sets, and the whole country seems to stay awake to celebrate the light.",
  "swedes:ritual": "On 26 June 1630, King Gustavus Adolphus landed with his army on the island of Usedom at Peenemünde to enter the Thirty Years' War. Contemporary accounts say that as soon as he stepped ashore, he knelt on the beach before his officers and prayed aloud for God's blessing on the campaign. When he saw them weeping he told them that the more one prays, the better one fights. The war ahead would decide Protestant Germany's future.",
  "swedes:rain": "In 1867 Sweden had a cold, wet spring and summer; frost and rain destroyed the harvest, and 1868 brought drought. Families in Småland and Värmland ate bark bread and watched the sky, waiting for a good year that did not come. Then they gave up waiting. Over the next decades more than a million Swedes emigrated to America, and Vilhelm Moberg's novels The Emigrants tell of the farmers who decided the world would not change unless they did.",
  "swedes:flood": "Imagined: In May the vårflod, the spring flood, comes down the great Norrland rivers. Snowmelt raises the Ångermanälven by metres, and the log drivers, flottare, ride timber down from the forests to the sawmills on the coast. A jam forms at a rapid; the water backs up and spills into a riverside village. The men walk out on the moving logs with long poles to break it loose, while their families pray on the bank.",
  "swedes:wind": "On 30 November 1700, the eighteen-year-old King Charles XII attacked a Russian army four times his size besieging Narva. At the moment of attack a snowstorm blew up, driving the snow straight into the faces of the Russians. The Swedes advanced in two columns under cover of the blizzard and broke through the entrenchments. The Russian army collapsed. Swedes believed God had sent the wind; Peter the Great learned from the defeat and rebuilt his army.",
  "swedes:fire": "On 7 May 1697 the old royal castle of Tre Kronor in Stockholm caught fire while the body of King Charles XI lay in state inside. Courtiers carried the king's coffin out through the smoke, and the archives, library and treasures burned. The fourteen-year-old Charles XII watched his childhood home disappear. On the same spot rose the present Royal Palace, but many of Sweden's medieval records were lost forever in that night.",
  "swedes:thunder": "In Swedish folk belief, thunder was Thor driving his goat-drawn wagon across the sky, and later the work of God against trolls and evil spirits. Farmers who found stone-age axes in their fields called them åskviggar, thunderbolts, believing lightning had left them in the ground. They kept them in the walls or under the threshold to protect the house from fire. When a summer storm came, the family sat still and waited for it to pass.",
  "swedes:birth": "In December 1626, Queen Maria Eleonora gave birth at Stockholm Castle. The baby was hairy and loud, and the midwives at first announced a boy. When the truth was found, no one dared tell the king, until his half-sister did. Gustavus Adolphus is said to have laughed: She will be clever, for she has deceived us all. The girl was Christina, who would become queen at six, rule, abdicate and astonish Europe.",
  "swedes:death": "On 30 November 1718, King Charles XII was shot through the head while inspecting trenches at the siege of Fredriksten in Norway. His soldiers carried his body back across the snowy mountains to Uddevalla on a stretcher, then onward to Stockholm, where he was buried in the Riddarholmen church. The painting by Gustaf Cederström, Bringing Home the Body of Karl XII, shows the grim march in snow, and Swedes still argue who fired the shot.",
  // ——— Lithuanians ———
  "lithuanians:hunt": "In the winter of 1409, preparing for war with the Teutonic Order, King Jogaila and Grand Duke Vytautas held a great hunt in the primeval forest of Białowieża. For weeks, hunters killed bison, aurochs, elk and deer, and the meat was salted, packed in barrels and floated down the Narew and Vistula to feed the army. It was a hunt with a war in mind. Next July that army fought at Grunwald.",
  "lithuanians:battle": "On 22 September 1236, near Saulė, Samogitian and Semigallian warriors caught the Livonian Brothers of the Sword returning from a raid. The knights were in heavy armour, and the marshy ground slowed their horses to a crawl. The lightly armed Lithuanians struck from all sides. The Master of the Order, Volkwin, and most of his knights were killed, and the Order was so broken that it had to join the Teutonic Knights. Lithuania still marks the day.",
  "lithuanians:gather": "Lithuanian women sang sutartinės, songs in canon, while scutching flax, weaving or harvesting together. Two or three groups sing short lines that overlap, so that neighbouring notes clash in deliberate seconds, sounding like bells or bees. The words are simple and old: the rue, the swan, the linen. The songs belonged to the work and to the gathering; without the others, a singer could not make the music at all.",
  "lithuanians:ritual": "Lithuania was the last pagan state in Europe, baptised only in 1387. Medieval chroniclers describe a perpetual fire kept for the gods in Vilnius, in the valley where the cathedral now stands. Priests tended it and cast lots to ask the gods about war and harvest; a Grand Duke would not march if the omens were bad. Archaeologists have found an altar beneath the cathedral floor, where the old and new faiths met.",
  "lithuanians:rain": "On 23 August 1989, about two million people from Lithuania, Latvia and Estonia joined hands in a human chain 675 kilometres long, from Vilnius through Riga to Tallinn. They called it the Baltic Way. It was fifty years since the Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact had given them to Stalin. They had waited half a century for the world to change; that evening they stood on the roadside singing, and seven months later Lithuania declared independence.",
  "lithuanians:flood": "Imagined: Every spring in the Nemunas delta, the river rises with snowmelt and ice from the east, and the island village of Rusnė is cut off by water. Families move their cows to the lofts and travel by boat to church and shop. Storks return to their nests on the roofs above the flooded meadows. The people of Rusnė do not fight the potvynis; they wait for it as for a season, knowing the water leaves rich soil behind.",
  "lithuanians:wind": "On the Curonian Spit, a narrow strip of sand between the Baltic and the lagoon, the forests were cut down in the 17th and 18th centuries. Storm winds then set the great dunes moving. Over the following decades, wandering dunes buried whole fishing villages, Nagliai, Karvaičiai and others, one by one, their churches and graves under the sand. The Curonian fishermen moved their homes again and again, until they finally planted the dunes with grass and pine.",
  "lithuanians:fire": "In 1336 the Teutonic Order besieged the hill-fort of Pilėnai. The defenders, under Duke Margiris, saw they could not hold. Rather than surrender and be baptised by force or enslaved, they threw their goods into a great fire, killed their families, and burned themselves with them. The Order's chronicler, Wigand of Marburg, recorded it with horror. For Lithuanians, Pilėnai became a symbol of choosing freedom over submission, at any price.",
  "lithuanians:thunder": "Perkūnas, the Lithuanian thunder god, struck with his axe at devils hiding in trees and stones. Oaks were sacred to him, and an oak struck by lightning was holy. When thunder rolled, people said Perkūnas was riding his fiery chariot over the clouds; to make the first thunder of spring bless the year, farmers rolled on the ground or knocked bread against the wall. Many still call a thunderstorm Perkūnija.",
  "lithuanians:birth": "Lithuanians believed that at birth Laima, goddess of fate, came to the cradle and decided the child's destiny: its happiness, its sorrows and the hour of its death. Nothing she spoke could be changed. Women gathered for the krikštynos, the naming feast, and the first bath of the baby was given with herbs and coins in the water. Everyone wished the child luck, knowing Laima had already spoken.",
  "lithuanians:death": "When Grand Duke Kęstutis was murdered in 1382, his body was burned in Vilnius in the old pagan way, as the Polish chronicler Jan Długosz relates: with his horse, his armour and weapons, his hounds and falcons, so that he would ride and hunt in the next world as he had in this one. The flames rose in the valley of the sacred fire. Lithuania mourned a warrior who had fought the Teutonic Order for fifty years.",
  // ——— Poles ———
  "poles:hunt": "In September 1752, King Augustus III held a great hunt in the forest of Białowieża. Beaters drove the animals into an enclosure where the court shot from a raised pavilion; 42 European bison, żubry, were killed, 20 of them by Queen Maria Józefa. A stone obelisk recording the tally still stands in the forest. Two centuries later the żubr was almost extinct, and Poles bred it back from a few zoo animals.",
  "poles:battle": "On 12 September 1683, King Jan III Sobieski led the relief of Vienna, besieged by the Ottoman army of Kara Mustafa. Late in the afternoon about eighteen thousand horsemen, with the winged hussars at their head, charged down the slopes of the Kahlenberg, the largest cavalry charge in history. The Ottoman camp broke. Sobieski wrote to the Pope: Venimus, vidimus, Deus vicit. We came, we saw, God conquered.",
  "poles:gather": "At the end of harvest, Polish villages hold dożynki. The last sheaf of grain is woven into a wreath decorated with flowers, apples and ribbons, carried in procession to church to be blessed, then presented to the lord of the manor or, today, the village head. Then the bread is shared, the band plays obereks and polkas, and everyone dances. The work of a whole summer becomes one evening of joy.",
  "poles:ritual": "Before dawn on 12 September 1683, on the Kahlenberg above besieged Vienna, the Capuchin friar Marco d'Aviano said Mass for the relief army in a small ruined chapel. King Jan III Sobieski served at the altar himself, and his son was knighted. The soldiers heard the Mass in silence, knowing that by evening Christendom might win Vienna or lose it. Then Sobieski mounted his horse and rode down to battle.",
  "poles:rain": "In August 1980, workers at the Lenin Shipyard in Gdańsk went on strike after the firing of the crane operator Anna Walentynowicz. Electrician Lech Wałęsa climbed the fence to lead them. For eighteen days the strikers waited inside the gates while families passed bread through the bars and priests said Mass in the yard. On 31 August the government signed. Solidarność was born, and Poland began to believe the world could change.",
  "poles:flood": "In July 1997, after days of heavy rain, the Odra and its tributaries rose in the Powódź tysiąclecia, the Millennium Flood. Opole, Racibórz and Kłodzko were swamped; in Wrocław students and residents filled sandbags around the university and the cathedral island through the night. Fifty-six people died in Poland. Neighbours ferried each other in canoes, and many still mark their houses with the line where the water stood.",
  "poles:wind": "In the Tatra highlands of Podhale, the halny is a warm, dry foehn wind that crashes down the northern slopes in autumn and spring. It can blow at a hundred kilometres an hour, flattening spruce forests and tearing roofs from wooden houses in Zakopane. Górale say the halny makes people restless and short-tempered. When it rises, the fiddlers stop playing and families close the shutters, waiting for the mountain to calm.",
  "poles:fire": "On 1 August 1944 the Home Army began the Warsaw Uprising against the German occupiers. For sixty-three days, fighters and civilians held out, passing through the sewers, while the Soviet army waited across the Vistula. After the surrender, Hitler ordered the city razed. German demolition squads burned and dynamited Warsaw street by street, until about 85 percent of the left-bank city lay in ruins. Poles rebuilt the Old Town brick by brick from old paintings.",
  "poles:thunder": "On 2 February, Candlemas, Polish Catholics bring thick candles to church to be blessed. This is the gromnica, the thunder candle, named after Matka Boża Gromniczna, Our Lady of the Thunder Candle, who in legend guards villages from wolves. During a storm, the gromnica is lit in the window to keep lightning from the house. It is also placed in the hands of the dying, so the same flame guards against the storm and lights the way to God.",
  "poles:birth": "On 19 February 1473, in the merchant city of Toruń on the Vistula, a son was born to a copper trader and his wife Barbara Watzenrode. He was named Mikołaj, after his father. His uncle, a bishop, paid for his education in Kraków and Italy, hoping he would rise in the Church. Instead, Nicolaus Copernicus quietly moved the Earth from the centre of the universe, and the world has not been the same since.",
  "poles:death": "Polish nobles of the 17th and 18th centuries were buried with great Baroque pomp. A portret trumienny, a coffin portrait painted on metal, was fixed to the coffin so the dead seemed to look at the mourners. The coffin stood under a castrum doloris, a catafalque hung with coats of arms. Accounts tell that an armoured horseman rode into the church and broke a lance, or fell from his horse, to show the knight had fallen.",
  // ——— Bohemians ———
  "bohemians:hunt": "Imagined: Křivoklát castle stands in the royal forests west of Prague, where Bohemian kings came to hunt deer and boar. On an autumn morning, the young Prince Wenceslas, later King Charles IV, rides out with huntsmen and hounds through the beech woods above the Berounka. Horns echo from the valley. Charles loved learning more than hunting, but a king of Bohemia had to know his forests, and the forests had to know their king.",
  "bohemians:battle": "On 14 July 1420, Crusaders summoned by Emperor Sigismund besieged Hussite Prague. On Vítkov Hill a few dozen Hussite men and women held a wooden stronghold against German knights. Then Jan Žižka, the one-eyed captain, struck from the flank, and the Praguers came up singing Ktož jsú boží bojovníci, Ye Who Are Warriors of God. The crusaders broke and ran. The hill is still called Žižkov, and the chorale became the Hussites' battle anthem.",
  "bohemians:gather": "On 9 July 1357, at 5:31 in the morning, Emperor Charles IV laid the foundation stone of the new stone bridge over the Vltava. Astrologers had chosen the moment because it formed a palindrome, 1357 9 7 5 31, and an auspicious alignment of planets. A crowd gathered on the riverbank to see it. The bridge, today's Charles Bridge, took half a century to finish and has carried Prague across its river ever since.",
  "bohemians:ritual": "The chronicler Cosmas of Prague, writing around 1120, tells how the princess Libuše, a seeress, stood on the rock of Vyšehrad above the Vltava and prophesied: I see a great city, whose glory will touch the stars. She sent men into the forest to find a man carving a threshold, práh, and there to build the city, Praha. Czechs have honoured the prophecy for centuries; Smetana made her vision the climax of his opera Libuše.",
  "bohemians:rain": "In November 1989, after police beat students on Národní street on the 17th, hundreds of thousands of Czechs filled Wenceslas Square in Prague day after day. They stood in the cold and rattled their keys, a sound like a bell telling the Communist government that it was time to go home. Václav Havel and Alexander Dubček spoke from a balcony. The world they had waited forty years to change changed in ten days.",
  "bohemians:flood": "In February 1342, a thaw sent ice floes and floodwater down the Vltava. The ice smashed the piers of the Judith Bridge, the old stone bridge that had joined the two halves of Prague since the 1170s, and most of it collapsed into the river. For fifteen years Praguers crossed by ferry or wooden footbridge, until Charles IV began the new bridge. The Vltava has risen many times since; in August 2002 it flooded the metro and Malá Strana.",
  "bohemians:wind": "Imagined: A summer gale sweeps across the Bohemian plateau from the west. In the Šumava forest, spruces snap like straw and whole hillsides fall at once. The glassmakers of the forest villages, who feed their furnaces with wood, look at the ruin and count it: firewood for years, but their forest gone. They carry the timber out for a whole season, and plant again, knowing they will never see it grown.",
  "bohemians:fire": "On 6 July 1415, at the Council of Constance, the Prague preacher Jan Hus was condemned as a heretic and burned at the stake for refusing to recant. He had been promised safe conduct by Emperor Sigismund. As the fire was lit he sang the Kyrie. His ashes were thrown into the Rhine so nothing could be kept as a relic. In Bohemia the news set the country ablaze in a different way: the Hussite revolution began.",
  "bohemians:thunder": "Imagined: Thunder over the Krkonoše, the Giant Mountains on Bohemia's northern border. The mountain folk say it is Krakonoš, the lord of the mountains, angry at someone who has mocked him or cheated a poor man. Lightning strikes a shepherd's hut on the ridge; the shepherd escapes. In the valley, his family whispers that he must have been honest after all, because Krakonoš spared him.",
  "bohemians:birth": "On 14 May 1316, Queen Elisabeth of Bohemia gave birth to a son in Prague. He was baptised Wenceslas, after the patron saint of the land, the hope of the last Přemyslid line now joined to the house of Luxembourg. His father, King John the Blind, sent him to France at seven, where he took the name Charles. As Emperor Charles IV he made Prague the capital of the Empire and founded its university.",
  "bohemians:death": "When Emperor Charles IV died in November 1378, Prague gave him the greatest funeral of the age. For eleven days his body lay in state at Prague Castle; then a procession of thousands, with hundreds of torchbearers and black-draped horses, carried him through the city to St Vitus Cathedral. Banners of all his lands were carried and lowered. Masters of the university he had founded called him the Father of the Fatherland, pater patriae.",
  // ——— Slavs ———
  "slavs:hunt": "Imagined: In the sixth century, in the marshy forests between the Dnieper and the Vistula, a Slavic family sets snares for hares and climbs to wild beehives in hollow trees, smoking out the bees for honey and wax. The men carry spears for the boar and the bear. Every hunt begins with a word to the forest spirit, the leshy, so that he will not lead them astray among the trees.",
  "slavs:battle": "Byzantine writers feared the Sclaveni. The Strategikon of Emperor Maurice, around 600, warns that they fought on foot with small shields and javelins, struck from forests and ravines, and hated open battle. When surprised, it says, they would sink into a river or pond and lie on the bottom for hours, breathing through long hollow reeds, so that no one could tell where the warriors were. Free men, the book admits, and hard to rule.",
  "slavs:gather": "The historian Procopius wrote around 550 that the Sclaveni and Antes were not ruled by one man but lived in what he called democracy, and decided all matters, good or bad, in common assembly. Picture the village gathered in a clearing, elders and householders arguing over war, planting and justice until they reached agreement. Later the Slavic veche, the town assembly, carried on the same habit for centuries.",
  "slavs:ritual": "At Arkona, on the island of Rügen, the Baltic Slavs kept the temple of Svantevit, the four-faced god. The chronicler Saxo Grammaticus describes how the priest asked the god about war: spears were laid on the ground in rows, and the god's white horse was led over them. If it stepped first with the right foot and touched no spear, the war would go well. The whole people waited on that horse's hooves.",
  "slavs:rain": "In times of drought, South Slavic villages held the dodola ritual. A girl was dressed from head to foot in green leaves and grass, and led from house to house by other girls singing to the sky for rain. At each house the woman of the home poured water over the dodola, and the girl turned around, spraying it back, as if she were the rain itself. Then everyone watched the clouds and waited.",
  "slavs:flood": "Imagined: Spring comes late to the Pripet marshes. When the snow melts all at once, the rivers spread out over the forest until villages stand on islands. Families carry grain and children into dugout boats and paddle to higher ground, driving the cattle ahead through shallow water. The old women say the water spirits, the vodyanoy and the rusalki, are taking their share, and throw a loaf of bread into the flood.",
  "slavs:wind": "Imagined: The Primary Chronicle names Stribog among the gods of the old Slavs, and later poets called the winds Stribog's grandsons. When a great autumn storm tears through the forest and lifts the thatch from the houses, the villagers say Stribog is angry. They hang a ribbon in a tree and throw flour into the wind to feed it, hoping it will pass over the fields and leave the grain.",
  "slavs:fire": "On the shortest night of the year, Kupala Night, Slavic young people gathered by rivers and lit great bonfires. Couples held hands and leapt over the flames together; if their hands did not part, their love would last. Girls floated wreaths of flowers with candles on the water, reading their futures in where they drifted. Legend says the fern blooms with a fiery flower only on that night, and whoever finds it will find treasure.",
  "slavs:thunder": "Around 550 Procopius wrote of the Slavs: they believe that one god, the maker of lightning, is the only lord of all things, and they sacrifice cattle and every kind of victim to him. That god was Perun, the thunderer, who strikes the serpent Veles with his axe. Oaks were his trees, Thursday his day. When thunder broke over the forest, the Slavs heard Perun's battle with the serpent rolling across the sky.",
  "slavs:birth": "Slavs believed that on the third night after a birth, three fate maidens, the Rozhanitsy or Sudenitsy, came invisibly to the cradle and declared the child's destiny: its wealth, its marriage, the time of its death. The mother laid out bread, salt and wine for them and kept a lamp burning. Relatives tried to stay awake and overhear the verdict. Some prayed the child would be the one to bring the family luck.",
  "slavs:death": "Early Slavs burned their dead on great pyres and raised mounds, kurgans, over the ashes, or placed them in urns on posts by the road. After the burning came the trizna, a funeral feast with games and mock battles in honour of the dead, and the strava, the meal shared at the graveside. Later, on Radonitsa and other days of the dead, families brought food to the graves, believing the ancestors came to share it.",
  // ——— Rus ———
  "rus:hunt": "Grand Prince Vladimir Monomakh, in the Instruction he wrote for his sons around 1117, boasted of his hunts: Two aurochs tossed me and my horse on their horns; a stag gored me; one elk trampled me, another butted me with its horns; a boar tore my sword from my thigh; a bear bit my saddle-cloth by my knee. He hunted himself, he said, and did not leave it to his servants. God, he added, kept me safe.",
  "rus:battle": "In 971 Prince Sviatoslav of Kiev was trapped by the Byzantine emperor John Tzimiskes at Dorostolon on the Danube. The Primary Chronicle gives his speech to his outnumbered men: Let us not disgrace the Rus land, but lie here as bones, for the dead know no shame. If we flee, we shall be shamed. Sviatoslav was the prince who warned his enemies before attacking: I am coming against you. To the Rus, that was honour.",
  "rus:gather": "In Novgorod, the great trading city of the Rus, free citizens were called to the veche, the town assembly, by ringing a special bell in Yaroslav's Court. They gathered in the open, shouted and argued, elected the city's posadnik and even its archbishop, and invited or expelled princes. The bell was the voice of the city's freedom, which is why Ivan III of Moscow took it away in 1478, when he ended Novgorod's independence.",
  "rus:ritual": "The Primary Chronicle says that in 987 Grand Prince Vladimir of Kiev sent envoys to examine the faiths of his neighbours, seeking the true God for his people. In Constantinople the envoys attended the liturgy in Hagia Sophia and reported: We knew not whether we were in heaven or on earth, for on earth there is no such splendour. In 988 Vladimir was baptised, and the people of Kiev were baptised in the Dnieper.",
  "rus:rain": "The Primary Chronicle tells that in 862 the tribes of the north, Slavs and Finns, were torn by feuds. Weary of strife, they sent across the sea to the Varangian Rus with this message: Our land is great and rich, but there is no order in it. Come and rule over us. Rurik and his brothers came. Whether legend or history, it records a people waiting and wishing for the world to settle into peace.",
  "rus:flood": "Imagined: Every April the Dnieper overflows at Kiev, the spring polovodye spreading the river across the meadows of the left bank until the far shore seems to vanish. Merchants who have wintered in the city prepare their boats, monoxyla carved from single trees, for the journey down to Constantinople. The high water carries them over the rapids that would otherwise wreck them. To the Rus, the flood is the start of the trading year.",
  "rus:wind": "In 860 a Rus fleet of about two hundred ships appeared before Constantinople, while the emperor was away, and plundered its suburbs. Patriarch Photius preached of terror in the city. Later Byzantine and Rus tradition said the patriarch dipped the robe of the Virgin into the sea, a sudden storm rose, and the Rus ships were smashed against the shore. The Rus who survived went home telling of the power of the Greeks' God.",
  "rus:fire": "In 945 the Drevlians killed Prince Igor of Kiev. His widow Olga took revenge. The Primary Chronicle says that in 946, after a long siege of Iskorosten, she asked for a modest tribute: three pigeons and three sparrows from every house. Her soldiers tied burning sulphur to the birds' feet and released them; they flew back to their nests under the eaves, and the whole town caught fire at once. Olga later became a Christian saint.",
  "rus:thunder": "Prince Vladimir set up an idol of Perun on the hill at Kiev in 980, with a silver head and a golden moustache. In 988, after his baptism, he ordered it tied to a horse's tail, beaten with sticks, and thrown into the Dnieper. The Primary Chronicle says that people wept as the thunder god floated downstream, and men ran along the bank pushing it off whenever it touched the shore, until it passed the rapids.",
  "rus:birth": "Imagined: In a timber house in Kiev, a Rus noblewoman gives birth during the long winter. The midwife ties the cord with a thread of flax, and the father, a Varangian merchant of the prince's druzhina, presses a small sword against the cradle so the boy will be brave. A priest will baptise him on the eighth day. One relative hopes he will defend Kiev against the steppe nomads; another fears he will die young doing it.",
  "rus:death": "In 922 the Arab envoy Ibn Fadlan watched the funeral of a Rus chieftain on the Volga. The dead man was laid in his ship, dressed in silk with food, drink and weapons beside him; a slave girl who chose to follow him was killed by an old woman called the Angel of Death. Then the ship was set on fire. A Rus man told Ibn Fadlan: You Arabs are fools. We burn him in a moment, and he goes straight to paradise.",
  // ——— Russians ———
  "russians:hunt": "Tsar Alexis Mikhailovich, who reigned 1645 to 1676, loved falconry above all pastimes. In 1656 he oversaw a book of rules for the hunt, the Uryadnik sokolnichya puti, setting out the ceremony by which a new falconer received his first bird. He kept thousands of hawks and falcons near Moscow and wrote to his chief falconer about their health. Falconry, he said, was a joy of the heart in a time of troubles.",
  "russians:battle": "On 8 September 1380, Grand Prince Dmitry of Moscow faced the Golden Horde army of Mamai at Kulikovo Field on the Don. By tradition the battle began with a duel: the monk Alexander Peresvet, sent by St Sergius, rode against the Tatar champion Chelubey, and both fell dead at the first clash. The battle that followed was terrible and long, but Dmitry won, and was called Donskoy. Russians count it the beginning of their liberation.",
  "russians:gather": "At Maslenitsa, Butter Week before Lent, Russians gather for the last feast before the fast. They eat stacks of blini, round and golden like the returning sun, go sledding down ice hills, hold fist fights and troika rides, and visit their in-laws. On the last day, Forgiveness Sunday, everyone asks each other for forgiveness, and a straw effigy of Lady Maslenitsa is burned to send winter away.",
  "russians:ritual": "In August 1380, before marching against Mamai, Grand Prince Dmitry rode to the Trinity monastery to ask the blessing of the abbot Sergius of Radonezh. Sergius prayed with him, and, the Life of Sergius says, told him: You will defeat your enemies. He sent two of his monks, Peresvet and Oslyabya, to fight beside him. For Russians, it is the image of a ruler asking God, through a holy man, what the future held.",
  "russians:rain": "In October 1480, Grand Prince Ivan III of Moscow and Khan Akhmat of the Great Horde faced each other across the river Ugra. Neither would cross. For weeks they waited, shouting insults over the water, while Ivan's clergy begged him to fight. Then winter came; the river began to freeze, and Akhmat, short of supplies, withdrew. The Great Stand on the Ugra ended the Tatar yoke without a battle. Russia changed by waiting.",
  "russians:flood": "On 7 November 1824, a storm from the Gulf of Finland drove the Neva backwards into St Petersburg. The water rose more than four metres; streets became rivers and houses floated. Hundreds drowned. Pushkin made it the setting of The Bronze Horseman: the clerk Yevgeny loses his beloved in the flood, goes mad, and shakes his fist at the statue of Peter the Great, who built the city in the marsh, and the statue gallops after him.",
  "russians:wind": "Imagined: On the Orenburg steppe in winter, the sky turns white and a buran, a blizzard, rises. A sledge with a coachman and a young officer loses the road within minutes; the horses stop, snow fills the air. The coachman says: It is a buran, master, we must wait. They sit wrapped in sheepskins listening to the wind howl, as Pushkin described in The Captain's Daughter, and pray to see a light.",
  "russians:fire": "On 14 September 1812, Napoleon's Grand Army entered a nearly empty Moscow. That night fires broke out everywhere; the governor, Count Rostopchin, had removed the fire pumps and, many believe, ordered the city burned. For four days the wooden city blazed, and three quarters of it was lost. Napoleon, watching from the Kremlin, said: What a people! These are Scythians. Russians gave up their holy city rather than surrender.",
  "russians:thunder": "In Russian folk belief, the prophet Elijah, Ilya, drove his fiery chariot across the sky, and thunder was the sound of its wheels. Ilyin Day, 2 August, was the day of storms; peasants did not work in the fields, for fear Ilya would strike them, and they finished the hay harvest before it. In a storm, families lit a candle before the icon and prayed, because lightning was God's punishment.",
  "russians:birth": "On 30 May 1672, in the Kremlin, Tsaritsa Natalya Naryshkina bore Tsar Alexis a son. The bells of Moscow rang for days, and the boy was baptised Peter in the Chudov Monastery. He was a youngest son and few expected him to rule; his half-brother's family plotted against him, and he grew up in fear. As Peter the Great he would drag Russia westward and build a new capital on the sea.",
  "russians:death": "In Russian Orthodox custom, the dead are washed and laid out at home, mirrors covered, a candle burning. The Psalter is read through the night. After burial come the remembrances on the third, ninth and fortieth days, when the soul is believed to pass toward judgement. Families eat kutya, sweet wheat with honey and raisins. On Radonitsa, after Easter, they visit the graves, share food with the dead and say Christ is risen.",
  // ——— Magyars ———
  "magyars:hunt": "Hungarian chronicles tell the legend of the Miraculous Stag. Two brothers, Hunor and Magor, sons of Ménrót, were hunting when a white stag appeared before them. They chased it for days across the marshes of the Maeotis, until it vanished in a land of rich pasture. There they found the daughters of a prince, married them, and became the ancestors of the Huns and the Magyars. The hunt led a people to its home.",
  "magyars:battle": "On 29 August 1526, at Mohács, the young King Louis II of Hungary met the army of Sultan Suleiman the Magnificent. The Hungarian heavy cavalry charged bravely, but the Ottoman cannon and janissaries tore it apart in under two hours. Seven bishops and most of the nobility fell. Louis, fleeing, drowned in the flooded Csele stream under his armoured horse. Hungarians still say, of a misfortune: more was lost at Mohács.",
  "magyars:gather": "The Gesta Hungarorum tells that before the Magyars entered the Carpathian Basin around 895, the seven chieftains of the tribes, the Hétmagyar, gathered and swore a blood oath. Each cut his arm and let the blood flow into one cup, and they chose Álmos, and his son Árpád after him, as their leader, swearing to share all they won. A people of seven tribes became one under that cup.",
  "magyars:ritual": "King Stephen I, who made Hungary Christian, had outlived his only son Imre, killed in a hunt in 1031. Tradition says that before he died in 1038, with no heir and fearing for the young kingdom, he raised the crown and offered Hungary to the Virgin Mary, asking her to protect it. Hungarians have called their country Regnum Marianum, Mary's Kingdom, ever since, and paintings show the old king kneeling with the crown in his hands.",
  "magyars:rain": "Imagined: On the Great Hungarian Plain in a summer drought, the herdsmen of the Hortobágy watch the sky. The wells dry; the grey cattle stand with lowered heads. On the horizon shimmers the délibáb, the mirage of lakes and towns floating in the air, cruel in its promise. The csikós rides out each morning to look west for clouds. When at last thunder sounds, the whole puszta seems to breathe again.",
  "magyars:wind": "Imagined: A summer storm sweeps across the puszta. With no tree for miles, the wind hits the horse herds head-on, and the csikós, the mounted herdsmen in their wide blue shirts, ride hard around the frightened horses to keep them from stampeding into the swamps. Hats fly, whips crack, and the herdsmen shout to one another through the rain. By dawn they count the herd and find, with luck, none lost.",
  "magyars:fire": "In September and October 1552, the small castle of Eger, under captain István Dobó, held out for 39 days against a huge Ottoman army. When the attackers climbed the walls, the defenders threw down fire-wheels, burning pitch and blazing barrels, and the women of Eger joined them, pouring boiling water and stones. Mines were countered and fires fought. The Ottomans withdrew. Géza Gárdonyi's novel Eclipse of the Crescent Moon made it every child's story.",
  "magyars:thunder": "Imagined: In the tenth century, before the conversion, a Magyar táltos, a shaman, climbs to a hilltop as a storm approaches the camp. Táltos were believed to fight in the clouds in the shape of bulls or flames, battling other spirits to decide whether hail would fall. The people below watch the lightning and listen to the thunder, waiting to know who won and whether their crops and horses will be spared.",
  "magyars:birth": "The chronicles tell that Emese, wife of the Magyar chief Ügyek, dreamed that a turul, a great falcon, came down upon her, and that from her womb flowed a river that went out over foreign lands, and from her line would come glorious kings. Her son was named Álmos, from álom, dream. His son Árpád led the Magyars into the Carpathian Basin and founded the dynasty that ruled Hungary for four hundred years.",
  "magyars:death": "In tenth-century Magyar graves, archaeologists find warriors buried with their horses in a special way: not the whole animal, but its hide, stuffed or folded, with the skull and the lower legs and hooves in place, laid by the rider's feet. Beside him are his bow, arrows, sabre and stirrups, sometimes a silver sabretache plate. The family believed the warrior would ride that horse into the other world, still a horseman.",
  // ——— Bulgarians ———
  "bulgarians:hunt": "Imagined: In the Rhodope mountains in autumn, Bulgarian shepherds and hunters climb the pine slopes after chamois and boar. The hunt is also a guard: bears and wolves threaten the flocks. A gaida, the goatskin bagpipe, plays in the evening by the fire, and an old hunter tells of Krali Marko, the hero who wrestled the mountain beasts. The meat is shared out, and the skins hung to dry in the sun.",
  "bulgarians:battle": "In July 811, Byzantine Emperor Nikephoros I invaded Bulgaria and burned Khan Krum's capital, Pliska. On his way home, his army was trapped in a mountain pass, the Varbitsa, blocked by wooden walls. At dawn on 26 July the Bulgarians attacked. Nikephoros was killed, the first emperor slain in battle in centuries. Krum, the chronicles say, had his skull lined with silver and drank from it with his nobles.",
  "bulgarians:gather": "On 1 March, Bulgarians give each other martenitsi, little tassels of twisted red and white wool, and say Chestita Baba Marta, Happy Grandmother March. Everyone wears them pinned to clothing or tied around the wrist until they see the first stork or swallow, or a blossoming tree. Then they tie the martenitsa to a branch. Whole cities bloom with red and white, wishing each other health for the coming spring.",
  "bulgarians:ritual": "In 866, the newly baptised Khan Boris I of Bulgaria sent Pope Nicholas I a list of 106 questions about how his people should live as Christians. Could they wear trousers? What should be done with the horse-tail standard they carried into battle? May they work on Sundays? The Pope answered each one. Boris wanted to know the future his people would have under the new God, and he asked with great seriousness.",
  "bulgarians:rain": "In summer droughts, Bulgarian villages held the peperuda, butterfly, ritual. A young girl, often an orphan, was dressed in a skirt of green leaves and vines and led from house to house by other children singing: Fly, fly, peperuda, give us rain. At each door the woman of the house poured water over her and gave flour or eggs. Then the village watched the sky, waiting for clouds to come over the mountains.",
  "bulgarians:flood": "Imagined: Spring on the lower Danube. The river rises with meltwater from half of Europe, spreading over the wetlands below Silistra and Svishtov. Fishermen move their boats to the willows; farmers drive cattle onto the high bank. In the Danube villages, people remember older floods and the ways their grandparents survived them, and they wait for the river to return, for it always leaves good fish and black soil behind.",
  "bulgarians:wind": "Imagined: A winter storm comes from the north across the Black Sea onto the Bulgarian coast. At Nesebar, on its rocky peninsula, waves break over the old walls and spray reaches the medieval churches. The fishermen pull their boats onto the shore and tie them down. In the church of St Stephen, an old woman lights candles to St Nicholas, protector of sailors, for the boats still out at sea.",
  "bulgarians:fire": "In the villages of the Strandzha mountains, on 21 May, the feast of Saints Constantine and Helena, the nestinari dance barefoot on glowing embers. Holding icons of the saints, they enter a trance to the sound of drum and bagpipe, step onto the coals and dance unburned. In the trance they were believed to speak prophecies for the village for the coming year. The custom is on UNESCO's list of intangible heritage.",
  "bulgarians:thunder": "Imagined: In Bulgarian villages, St Elijah, Sveti Ilia, was the master of thunder and hail, and his feast on 20 July was a day of fear and respect. No one worked the fields that day. When a black storm gathers over the Balkan range, an old woman takes the bread blessed at Easter and sets it on the threshold, crossing herself and praying the hail will pass the ripening wheat.",
  "bulgarians:birth": "In Bulgarian folk belief, on the third night after a child's birth, the orisnitsi, three fate-maidens, came to the house to decide its fate. The family prepared a table with bread, honey and wine, swept the floor and left the door unlocked. The mother and grandmother waited, hoping to dream the verdict. A child might become a great hero or die young; the orisnitsi did not tell, and no one could change their words.",
  "bulgarians:death": "In 1014, after the battle of Kleidion, the Byzantine emperor Basil II blinded about fifteen thousand Bulgarian captives, leaving one in every hundred with one eye to lead the others home. When the blinded army reached Prilep, Tsar Samuel saw them and collapsed, and died two days later. Bulgarian memory has kept it as a story of a tsar whose heart broke for his soldiers, a grief rather than a defeat.",
  // ——— Byzantines ———
  "byzantines:hunt": "Imagined: In the hills of Bithynia across the Bosporus, a Byzantine nobleman rides out with a trained cheetah, a pardos, sitting behind him on the horse's rump, hooded like a falcon. Beaters drive a gazelle out of the brush; the hood comes off, the cat leaps down and runs. Servants with hounds and hawks follow. In the evening there is wine, music and talk of the court, and the city's domes glitter across the water.",
  "byzantines:battle": "In the early hours of 29 May 1453, the Ottoman army of Mehmed II stormed the walls of Constantinople. The last emperor, Constantine XI Palaiologos, had taken communion in Hagia Sophia the evening before and asked forgiveness of everyone. When the Ottomans broke through, he threw off his imperial insignia, it is said, and plunged into the fighting as a common soldier. His body was never surely identified. The empire of a thousand years ended that morning.",
  "byzantines:gather": "The Hippodrome of Constantinople held perhaps a hundred thousand people for the chariot races, divided between the Blues and the Greens. In January 532 the two factions united against Justinian, shouting Nika!, Conquer!, and set the city on fire. Justinian prepared to flee, but Empress Theodora, Procopius writes, said that royalty is a fine burial shroud. The emperor stayed; troops trapped the crowd in the Hippodrome and some thirty thousand died. From the ashes rose the new Hagia Sophia.",
  "byzantines:ritual": "In the summer of 626, Avars, Slavs and Persians besieged Constantinople while Emperor Heraclius was far away in the east. Patriarch Sergius carried the icon of the Virgin around the walls in procession, and the people prayed to her day and night. When the enemy fleet was destroyed and the Avars withdrew, the city gave thanks standing all night in church, singing the Akathist hymn, the hymn sung without sitting, to the Theotokos, their protector.",
  "byzantines:rain": "In the spring of 1453, besieged Constantinople waited for the Venetian fleet that the West had promised. On 3 May a small ship slipped past the Ottomans to look for it. It searched the Aegean for three weeks and found nothing. On 23 May the sailors came back with the news, knowing they were returning to almost certain death. They said they had come back because it was their duty. The city understood that no help was coming.",
  "byzantines:flood": "In April 525, the river Daisan burst through the walls of Edessa in Mesopotamia, one of the great Christian cities of the empire. The chronicles say that the water swept away houses, churches and baths, and thousands drowned in the night. Emperor Justin I sent money to rebuild, and the city took the name Justinopolis in gratitude. Engineers dug a new channel to turn the river around the walls, so it could never return.",
  "byzantines:wind": "Imagined: In late autumn, the grain fleet from Alexandria is overdue. A storm from the north has been blowing for days down the Bosporus and across the Sea of Marmara, and the people of Constantinople, who live on Egyptian bread, watch from the sea walls for sails. The bakers have cut the loaves smaller. In the churches they pray to St Nicholas, patron of sailors. When at last the ships appear, the city cheers.",
  "byzantines:fire": "In the 670s, when Arab fleets besieged Constantinople, a refugee architect from Syria named Kallinikos brought the Byzantines a new weapon: a liquid fire pumped through bronze siphons from the bows of their ships. It burned even on water and could not be put out with it. The Arab fleet was destroyed. The recipe for Greek fire, the sea fire, was kept a state secret so well that it is still unknown today.",
  "byzantines:thunder": "On 25 May 1453, during the siege, the people of Constantinople carried the icon of the Hodegetria, the Virgin, through the streets to pray for the city. Suddenly the icon slipped from its bearers and fell, and could hardly be lifted. Then a violent thunderstorm and hail struck, so heavy that children were nearly swept away. The next day a thick fog covered the city. Many took these as signs that God had withdrawn His protection.",
  "byzantines:birth": "Imagined: In a house near the Forum of Constantine, a merchant's wife is in labour. The midwife has hung an amulet of the Virgin and St Anne by the bed, and the family priest waits with oil. The baby girl cries; on the eighth day she will be named, on the fortieth she will be brought to church with her mother. Her father hopes she will marry well; her grandmother, who survived the plague, prays simply that she will live.",
  "byzantines:death": "Byzantines mourned with lamentation and hope. The body was washed, dressed and carried to church, and the mourners gave the last kiss while singing Come, let us give the last kiss. Kollyva, boiled wheat with honey and nuts, was shared on the third, ninth and fortieth days, a sign of resurrection. For centuries emperors were laid in porphyry sarcophagi in the Church of the Holy Apostles, near the tomb of Constantine the Great.",
  // ——— Macedonian Dynasty ———
  "macedonian-dynasty:hunt": "In August 886, Basil I, the Armenian peasant who had risen to found the Macedonian dynasty, was hunting in Thrace when a great stag turned on him. The chroniclers say its antlers caught his belt and it dragged him from his horse into the forest, until a servant cut the belt with a sword. The old emperor, suspecting a plot, had the servant punished for drawing a blade against him, and died of his injuries days later.",
  "macedonian-dynasty:battle": "In 960 the general Nikephoros Phokas sailed to Crete, held by Arab emirs for over a century, with a vast fleet carrying Greek fire. He landed in full armour, his cavalry riding straight off ramps from the ships. Through the winter he besieged the fortress of Chandax, and on 7 March 961 it fell. Constantinople celebrated with a triumph in the Hippodrome. Nine years later Nikephoros himself was emperor.",
  "macedonian-dynasty:gather": "In 949, Liudprand of Cremona, an envoy from Italy, was received by Emperor Constantine VII at the Magnaura palace. He describes golden lions that roared and beat their tails, mechanical birds singing in a golden tree, and the throne which, while he bowed, rose suddenly toward the ceiling, so that when he looked up the emperor sat far above him in new robes. The whole court gathered to watch the foreigners' amazement.",
  "macedonian-dynasty:ritual": "Emperor Leo VI, called the Wise, was so learned that later generations credited him with the Oracles of Leo the Wise, a book of riddles and pictures said to foretell the emperors and the fate of Constantinople. Byzantines consulted them in times of crisis, reading in their cryptic verses the coming of rulers and the fall or salvation of the City. They believed the future was written, and that the wise could read it in solemn silence.",
  "macedonian-dynasty:rain": "The chroniclers write that in the winter of 927 to 928 the ground of the empire froze hard for 120 days. Crops failed and a great famine followed. Emperor Romanos Lekapenos, ruling beside the young Constantine VII, had wooden shelters built under the porticoes of Constantinople for the poor and gave out food and money each month. The people waited for spring, and for the land to forgive them.",
  "macedonian-dynasty:flood": "Imagined: In the tenth century the river Sangarios in Bithynia rises after weeks of rain and pours over the plain below Justinian's great stone bridge. The bridge holds, as its builders promised four centuries before, but the villages around it are flooded. Peasants crowd onto the bridge with goats and grain sacks while monks from a nearby monastery row out to fetch the stranded, singing psalms as they pull.",
  "macedonian-dynasty:wind": "Imagined: An imperial fleet sailing from the Golden Horn to fight Arab corsairs is struck by a gale in the Aegean. Dromons lose oars and masts, and the Greek fire siphons are lashed down so they cannot break loose. The admiral, kneeling on the deck, vows a church to the Virgin if they survive. Three days later the battered fleet limps into Naxos, and the sailors give thanks on the shore.",
  "macedonian-dynasty:fire": "In June 941, Prince Igor of Kiev attacked Constantinople with a great fleet. The emperor Romanos Lekapenos had only fifteen old ships in the city, which he fitted with Greek fire siphons on the bow, the stern and both sides. The patrikios Theophanes led them out. When the Rus closed in, the fire poured onto their boats; Rus warriors leapt into the sea in their armour. Survivors told Kiev that the Greeks had lightning from heaven.",
  "macedonian-dynasty:thunder": "Imagined: A summer thunderstorm breaks over Constantinople during the feast of the Prophet Elijah. Lightning strikes the bronze statue on a column in the Forum, and crowds gather to see the blackened metal. Some say it is a portent for the emperor; others, more practical, point at the sky and hurry home. In the Great Palace, clerks note the event in the chronicle, so that wise men may interpret it.",
  "macedonian-dynasty:birth": "In September 905, Zoe Karbonopsina, the mistress of Emperor Leo VI, gave birth to a son in the Porphyra, the purple-walled chamber of the palace where imperial children were born. The boy was Constantine VII, called Porphyrogennetos, born in the purple. His parents were not married, and the Church protested when Leo married Zoe as his fourth wife. Many feared the child would cause schism; instead he became a scholar-emperor.",
  "macedonian-dynasty:death": "Basil II, the Bulgar-slayer, died on 15 December 1025 after nearly fifty years of rule. He chose not to be buried among the emperors at the Holy Apostles, but in the church of St John the Theologian at the Hebdomon, outside the walls, among his soldiers' parade ground. His epitaph says: No one saw my spear lie idle. I guarded the children of the New Rome. Soldier to the end, he wished to rest where the army mustered.",
});
